  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe picture perfect stretch of Fall weather will continue for a\nfew more days, but a changing mid/upper air pattern will result\nin warming temperatures. The persistent surface high over the\nGreat Lakes will drift into the Mid Atlantic today as the big\nEast Coast low finally weakens and ejects toward Nova Scotia.\nThis will allow the mid/upper ridge over the center of the\ncountry to build east across the Ohio Valley and eastern Great\nLakes today through Thursday, eventually resulting in a strong \neastern CONUS mid/upper ridge and strong surface high over the \nCarolinas and western Atlantic by Thursday. At the same time, a\nclosed mid/upper low over the Desert Southwest today will slowly\nlift into the central Plains by Thursday and begin to phase\nwith a deepening northern stream mid/upper trough over the\nnorthern Rockies and northern Plains. \n\nThis set up will result in deepening southerly flow and\nresultant warm air advection. Highs will warm into the low/mid\n70s areawide today, then mid/upper 70s Wednesday. Upper 70s/low\n80s are expected by Thursday, and it will be noticeably more\nhumid, with dew points in the low/mid 60s. \n\nMuch of this time period through Thursday should stay dry with\njust increasing clouds Wednesday and Thursday, but increasing\nmoisture advection and isentropic ascent ahead of a cold front\nslowly sinking south through the central Great Lakes in \nresponse to the digging trough could lead to some rain showers \nin the far northern counties Wednesday night. The NAMNest and \nRRFS bring some showers across the area Wednesday night into \nThursday morning, while other CAMS, the NAM12, ECMWF, and GFS \nremain mainly dry. At this time, stuck with dry NBM POPS \nWednesday night into Thursday morning, but may need to \neventually introduce some showers if the trend continues. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe aforementioned cold front will begin to sag south through\nthe region Thursday night and Friday. The front will be very\nslow to progress through due to the aforementioned closed low\nphasing with the digging northern stream trough, resulting in a\nweak surface wave on the front Thursday night and Friday. This\nsurface wave will have synoptic support from the right entrance\nregion of a 100-120+ knot H3 jet streak over Lower Michigan, so\nexpect enhanced moisture advection and isentropic ascent along\nthe front to drive a band of widespread showers. Isolated\nthunder cannot be ruled out, but given the time of year and very\nlittle instability projected, this will mainly be a stratiform\nrain event. Early QPF estimates give 0.25 to 0.75 inches across\nthe region, so no flooding is expected. The greatest\nprobabilities to exceed 1 inch appear to be in NW Ohio and near\nthe lakeshore areas. The clouds and precip will hold\ntemperatures down Friday, so expect highs in the upper 60s/low\n70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nCanadian high pressure building down across the Great Lakes in\nthe wake of the cold front will allow the area to dry out Friday\nnight and Saturday, but it will be much cooler as the longwave\nmid/upper trough carves out across the northern Plains, Great\nLakes, and Northeast. Highs Saturday will only reach the mid\n60s. A strong, reinforcing shortwave trough dropping through the\nbroader longwave trough Sunday will further deepen the broad\ntrough and push another cold front through the region. Showers\nare expected with the frontal passage, but the shortwave and \ndeepening trough may lead to cyclogenesis over the \nAppalachians/Mid Atlantic that would have a larger shield of \nrain. Confidence is low 5 days out, so NBM POPS look reasonable\nSunday, with the highest chances in eastern Ohio and western \nPA. Nevertheless, it will dry out Sunday night and Monday, but\nsome of the coolest temperatures of the season so far are\nexpected as an impressive 1035 mb surface high drops into the\nUpper Midwest. Highs Monday will only be in the upper 50s/low \n60s. This could set up frost potential early next week.",
